statement.p:
In each of the following problems, give an example of a function f: [n] \rightarrow [m] of the specified type. You get to pick the values for n and m. Describe your function by using a picture. Then decide if there is a required relationship between n and m, the sizes of the domain and the codomain (also called the "target").









 Create a function f: [n] \rightarrow [m] that is injective (one-to-one) but NOT surjective (onto). What is the required relationship between n and m? (One of: n  >m or n < m or n = m or "none.")








\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.8]


\draw (0,0) ellipse (1 and 1.75);

\draw (0,1) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 1}};
\draw (0,0) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 2}};
\draw (0,-1) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 3}};

\draw (0,-2.5) node {X = [3]};


\draw (4,0) ellipse (1 and 2.25);

\draw (4,1.5) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 1}};
\draw (4,.5) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 2}};
\draw (4,-.5) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 3}};
\draw (4,-1.5) node[circle,draw] {{\tiny 4}};



\draw[arrows={-latex}, very thick] (.25,1) -- (3.8,.6);
\draw[arrows={-latex}, very thick] (.25,0) -- (3.8,-.4);
\draw[arrows={-latex}, very thick] (.25,-1) -- (3.75,-1.5);

\draw (4,-2.75) node {Y= [4]};

\end{tikzpicture}



{Required Relationship: n < m}










 Create a function f: [n] \rightarrow [m] that is injective (one-to-one) and surjective (onto). What is the required relationship between n and m? (One of: n  >m or n < m or n = m or "none.")







