Required Relationship: none

Let f: \R \times \R \rightarrow \R \times \R be defined by f(x,y) = (2y, x-y). Is f injective? surjective? Justify your answer with either a proof or a counterexample.





The function f is injective. Suppose that f(x_1, y_1) = f(x_2, y_2). This means that (2y_1, x_1-y_1) = (2y_2, x_2 - y_2). We  find that
\begin{eqnarray*}
2y_1 &=& 2y_2 \\
y_1 &=& y_2
\end{eqnarray*}
and then
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_1 - y_1 &=& x_2 - y_2 \\
x_1 - y_1 &=& x_2 - y_1 \\
x_1 &=& x_2
\end{eqnarray*}
Therefore (x_1, y_1) = (x_2, y_2).



The function f is surjective. Pick any (a,b) \in \R \times \R). We must find (x,y) \in \R \times \R such that f(x,y)=(a,b). In order for this to hold, we need
\begin{eqnarray*}
2y &=& a \\
x-y &=& b
\end{eqnarray*}
Solving this linear systems gives x= b+ \frac{a}{2} and y=\frac{a}{2}. We can then check that

f \left(b+ \frac{a}{2},  \frac{a}{2} \right) = \left( 2 \cdot \frac{a}{2}, b+ \frac{a}{2} - \frac{a}{2} \right) = (a,b).
